Interface Segregation Principle says interfaces should be small and specific so classes don’t implement methods they don’t use.

Split large, general-purpose interfaces into smaller, more specific ones.
Example: Instead of a single Worker interface with unrelated methods, use separate interfaces for each responsibility.


    Show ISP violation: a single interface forces classes to implement methods they don’t need.
    Example: Worker interface with work() and eat(). Robot must implement eat(), which is irrelevant.

    Fix: Split into Workable and Eatable interfaces. Human implements both. Robot only implements Workable.
    Use industry standard code: interfaces for behaviors, classes implement only what they need.

Violation Example (‘Worker’ interface):

    public interface Worker {
        void work();
        void eat();
    }


    public class Human implements Worker {

        @Override
        public void work() {
            System.out.println("Human is working");
        }

        @Override
        public void eat() {
            System.out.println("Human is eating");
        }
    }


    // Java
    public class Robot implements Worker {

        @Override
        public void work() {
            System.out.println("Robot is working");
        }

        @Override
        public void eat() {
            // Irrelevant for Robot, but must implement
            throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Robot can't eat");
        }
    }


Industry Standard Fix (Split interfaces):

    public interface Workable {
        void work();
    }


    public interface Eatable {
        void eat();
    }


    
    public class Human implements Workable, Eatable {

        @Override
        public void work() {
            System.out.println("Human is working");
        }

        @Override
        public void eat() {
            System.out.println("Human is eating");
        }
    }


    
    public class Robot implements Workable {

        @Override
        public void work() {
            System.out.println("Robot is working");
        }
    }

    
    public class Robot implements Workable {

        @Override
        public void work() {
            System.out.println("Robot is working");
        }
    }

The above fix follows the interface segregation principle : Classes only depend on interfaces relevant to their behavious.